#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:150
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:618
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:831
msgid "To properly use this method, it is important to identify three things:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:152
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:620
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:833
msgid "Which chat_template would you use?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:154
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:622
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:835
msgid ""
"What are the keys in your dataset, and what are the possible roles? For "
"example, in OpenAI format, the keys would be messages, role, and content,"
" respectively, whereas the possible roles are system, user, and "
"assistant."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:156
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:624
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:837
msgid ""
"What do you want to mask? For instance, only assistant messages, only "
"last message, or nothing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:158
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:626
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:839
msgid ""
"There are a lot of chat_templates out there. Axolotl supports the common "
"ones: supported chat templates. For example, to use ChatML, it would be "
"chat_template: chatml."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:160
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:628
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:841
msgid ""
"However, it is also possible to use the already configured template "
"within the tokenizer by specifying chat_template: tokenizer_default. If "
"you want a fallback (in case some tokenizer does not have it pre-"
"configured), you can do chat_template: tokenizer_default_fallback_chatml "
"to fallback to the ChatML template if a tokenizer template was not found."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:162
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:630
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:843
msgid ""
"One last but powerful approach is to bring your own template. This can be"
" set via:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:164
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:632
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:845
msgid ""
"We currently default to OpenAI format for dataset keys, so if that’s your"
" current dataset format, there’s nothing to do here."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:166
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:634
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:847
msgid ""
"If your dataset format is different, here are the keys you should check "
"(with their defaults):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:168
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:636
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:849
msgid ""
"In some chat_templates (e.g. Gemma), the roles are hardcoded to user and "
"assistant. Consequently, you may find it necessary to map the roles in "
"your dataset to these above. We currently have some defaults that should "
"work for common datasets, but if you get a KeyError, it would be "
"necessary to add mapping for your roles. Here is an example of how it "
"would look like:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:170
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:638
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:851
msgid ""
"In the example above, all gpt and model values are converted to "
"assistant. All human values are converted to user."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:172
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:640
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:853
msgid ""
"The common use case for chat_template is for chat messages, therefore, it"
" is common to mask all non-assistant messages. Assistant messages refer "
"to the bot messages that you want the model to learn on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:174
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:642
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:855
msgid "To train on all assistant messages, you would set the following configs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:176
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:644
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:857
msgid ""
"The train_on_eos config means that it would mask all EOS tokens for turns"
" that aren’t assistant-turns. The other options are: all and last to "
"choose which EOS to train on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:178
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:646
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:859
msgid ""
"Perhaps, you want to train on assistant and narrator roles, you can "
"simply add narrator to the list of roles_to_train. You would also need to"
" add it to the mapping of roles above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:180
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:648
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:861
msgid ""
"As chat_templates may use hardcoded EOS/EOT tokens that are different "
"from the tokenizer’s EOS, it is highly recommended to set them. For "
"example, ChatML uses <|im_end|> to end turns."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:182
#: ../../source/references_dataset-formats.md:650
